Based on my research, I found that there appears to be some confusion around the exact flag name. The llama.cpp documentation specifically mentions `--spec-draft-n-cpu-moe` (or `-ncmoed`) which is used to specify how many layers of Mixture of Experts (MoE) weights should be kept in CPU memory for the draft model during speculative decoding.

The flag helps optimize performance when running MoE-based models by selectively keeping certain expert layers on CPU memory, particularly for the draft model used in speculative decoding. This allows partial offloading of MoE weights to CPU to improve compatibility or performance characteristics with large language models that utilize expert layers.

However, I couldn't find specific documentation for a flag simply named `--n-cpu-moe` - it appears this might be an incorrect or simplified name for the actual `--spec-draft-n-cpu-moe` flag.
